  with no build step.

  [1mMy recommendation is B.[0m You picked the strict redaction option, which tells me correctness there matters more than
  file count — and a pure core is what makes that rule testable and keeps the "never accept a raw password" guarantee in
  one reviewable place. C's costs land on files that have nothing to do with logging, which is the opposite of what I'd
  want from a subsystem addition.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which architecture should the design use? → B: core + adapters (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Approach B it is.

  One more thing to settle before I write the design: this repo has no tooling configured at all — no test runner, no
  linter, no [38;5;153mscripts[39m block, no dependencies. Setting that up is cheapest now, before the logger exists, and it's most
  valuable here specifically because the redaction rules are the kind of thing you want a test to catch rather than a
  code review.

  Worth knowing: Node ships a built-in test runner ([38;5;153mnode:test[39m) and assertion library, so unit tests cost zero
  dependencies and keep the repo's current zero-dep property intact. A linter and end-to-end browser tests both mean
  real [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m, which is a genuine change to what this project is.

